 First we need the matrix product of the two matrices. If we label the matrices $A$ and $B$, then we have: 
\begin{align*}
A = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 \\
                   5 & -3
                      \end{pmatrix} \qquad B = \begin{pmatrix} 
                             8 & -2 \\
                             1 & 1
                                \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
So matrix multiplication would be: $AB$, and we compute to get:
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix} 
            2 & 0 \\ 
            5 & -3
                \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 
            8 & -2 \\ 
            1 & 1
                \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 
            16 & -4 \\ 
            37 & -13
                \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*} 
otherwise: $BA$, and we compute to get:
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix} 
            8 & -2\\ 
            1 & 1
                \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 
            2 & 0 \\ 
            5 & -3
                \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 
            1 & 6\\ 
            7 & -3
                \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
As you can see, we could do it either way, as matrix multiplication is not commutative, and hence the two ways give different results. 
However, if we choose to do $BA$, we can check to see if we can simplify this system by using row reduction.  
